<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ellora Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 600–<a href="/years/1000">1000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Elapura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ellora Caves are a group of 34 rock-cut Buddhist, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Jain temples and monasteries near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, carved between about 600 and 1000 CE. The most famous, the Kailasa temple, was cut from a single mass of rock under the Rashtrakuta king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> I in the 8th century. They became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1983.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ellora-caves.jpg" alt="Ellora Caves" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ellora_Caves,_India,_Kailasanatha_Temple_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Ellora Caves are cut into the basalt cliffs of the Charanandri Hills, about 29 km north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a>. The 34 caves open to the public are 12 Buddhist (caves 1 to 12), 17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> (caves 13 to 29) and 5 Jain (caves 30 to 34), and several of them are not strictly caves, since they have no roof. Eleven of the Buddhist caves are viharas, or monasteries with prayer halls. Cave 10, the Vishvakarma or Carpenter's cave, is a chaitya worship hall whose rock is finished to look like wooden beams, with a seated Buddha about 15 feet high. The Jain caves belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> sect and stress the twenty-four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">tirthankaras</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars disagree about the order of construction, in particular whether the Buddhist or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> caves were carved first. The broad consensus described by Geri Hockfield Malandra and others sees a Buddhist phase from about 600 to 730 CE and a later Hindu and Jain phase from about 730 to 950 CE. The Rashtrakuta dynasty, which ruled from 753 to 982, built part of the Hindu and Buddhist caves. Funding came from royalty, traders and wealthy people of the region.</p><p>Cave 16, the Kailasha temple, is dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and modelled on Mount Kailasha. Gateway, assembly hall, multi-storey main temple, surrounding shrines and a spire are all carved from one rock, and the work was completed in the 8th century with the support of King Krishna I. The Baroda inscription of 812 mentions that a great edifice was built on a hill by Krishnaraja at Elapura. Shrines in the complex honour Ganga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a>, the ten avatars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and many other deities, and relief panels summarise the two great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> epics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> caves were dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and have a rock-cut linga-yoni within the shrine, surrounded by space for circumambulation. Cave 29, Dhumar Lena, was built around a natural waterfall, described as falling over Shiva's brow in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> season. Cave 21 holds a large display of the seven mother goddesses. The caves served as temples and monasteries and as a rest stop for pilgrims, and the site also lay on an ancient trade route, which made it an important commercial centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Today the Ellora Caves, with the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves</a>, are a protected monument under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ellora Caves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chalukya builders drew on the Pallava Kailasanatha temple at Kanchipuram, and their work in turn shaped the Rashtrakuta dynasty, whose rock-cut Kailasa temple at <b>Ellora</b> is often compared with the Virupaksha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The founder of the imperial line, Dantidurga, built up his power around <b>Ellora</b> and the upper Godavari valley, and his inscriptions claim campaigns in Gujarat, Malwa and Kosala; his record is carved in the Dashavatara cave at Ellora.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Some historians attribute the great Shiva cave at Elephanta and early caves at <b>Ellora</b> to their patronage, though this is debated.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalachuris-of-mahishmati">Kalachuris of Mahishmati</a></li></ul></section>
